-- Q3361: Stock Compensation and Business Combinations [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Liability awards remeasured at fair value each reporting period","Equity awards measured only at the grant date fair value","Revenue recognized over the vesting period of the SARs","Off-balance-sheet items disclosed only in the footnotes"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 718, cash-settled SARs are classified as liability awards. The liability is remeasured at fair value each reporting period until settlement, with changes recognized as compensation expense. This differs from equity-classified awards, which are measured only at grant date.
Wrong (B): Equity classification applies to awards settled in shares, not cash-settled SARs that create an obligation to pay cash.
Wrong (C): Stock compensation is an expense, not revenue; SARs create compensation cost recognized over the service period.
Wrong (D): Cash-settled SARs must be recognized on the balance sheet as a liability, not disclosed off-balance-sheet.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3361;

-- Q3363: Stock Compensation and Business Combinations [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Full fair value of the options recognized over the service period","Zero because the performance condition is not yet probable of achievement","Half the fair value recorded as a conservative estimate of vesting","The intrinsic value of the options at the reporting date"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 718, when a performance condition is not probable of achievement, no compensation expense is recognized. Once the condition becomes probable, cumulative catch-up expense is recognized for the elapsed portion of the service period.
Wrong (A): Full fair value recognition requires the performance condition to be probable; expense is deferred until that threshold is met.
Wrong (C): ASC 718 does not permit partial recognition based on conservative estimates; it is all-or-nothing based on probability.
Wrong (D): Intrinsic value is not the measurement basis for stock options under ASC 718; fair value at grant date is used.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3363;

-- Q3365: Stock Compensation and Business Combinations [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only the Black-Scholes model regardless of available data","The vesting period only as the best estimate of expected term","The contractual term only as the maximum possible exercise period","The simplified method using the midpoint of vesting and contractual term"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 718 and SEC Staff Accounting Bulletin guidance, the simplified method estimates expected term as (vesting period + contractual term) / 2. This is available when a company lacks sufficient historical exercise data. For example, options vesting in 4 years with a 10-year term yield (4+10)/2 = 7 years.
Wrong (A): Black-Scholes is a valuation model, not a method for estimating expected term; the expected term is an input to Black-Scholes.
Wrong (B): Using only the vesting period would understate the expected term since employees typically hold options beyond the vesting date.
Wrong (C): Using only the contractual term would overstate the expected term since most employees exercise before expiration.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3365;

-- Q3367: Stock Compensation and Business Combinations [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The contingency is assessed as remote under ASC 450 probability thresholds","The fair value of the contingent liability can be determined at acquisition date","Only after the contingency is fully resolved and the outcome is certain","Contingent liabilities are never recognized in a business combination transaction"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 805, contingent liabilities assumed in a business combination are recognized at fair value at the acquisition date if fair value can be determined. This is a lower threshold than ASC 450, which generally requires the loss to be probable. The fair value may reflect probability and magnitude of potential outcomes.
Wrong (A): Remote contingencies are not recognized under ASC 450, but ASC 805 uses a fair value threshold, not a probability threshold.
Wrong (C): ASC 805 does not require resolution before recognition; fair value measurement at acquisition date is sufficient.
Wrong (D): ASC 805 explicitly requires recognition of contingent liabilities when fair value is determinable.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3367;

-- Q3371: Advanced Consolidations [medium/L2]
